{
  "/do-motor-dealers-need-fca-authorisation": {
    "title": "Do Motor Dealers Need FCA Authorisation?",
    "answer": "Usually, yes, if you introduce customers to regulated car finance: you need the relevant Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) authorisation or a valid appointed-representative arrangement covering that activity. Selling vehicles for cash alone does not require credit-broking permission. Check the actual activities and any applicable exclusions before starting; using another finance broker does not automatically remove your own responsibilities.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Start with what your dealership does",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Map the journey from the customer’s first finance enquiry to the lender or broker. Include online enquiries, telephone introductions, showroom conversations and any help with settling finance on a part-exchange. A label such as “we only introduce” does not settle the regulatory position."
        ],
        "bullets": [],
        "columns": [
          "Dealership activity",
          "What to investigate"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Selling vehicles without arranging or introducing finance",
            "Vehicle sales alone do not establish a need for credit broking permission. Check any other activities separately."
          ],
          [
            "Introducing customers to lenders or another credit broker",
            "Assess credit broking and the permissions or principal arrangement needed."
          ],
          [
            "Introducing customers to a consumer hire provider",
            "Check the scope for consumer hire introductions; do not assume it is the same as selling a car for cash."
          ],
          [
            "Helping settle finance on a part-exchange",
            "Debt adjusting and debt counselling permissions may also be relevant."
          ],
          [
            "Providing credit or hiring out your own vehicles",
            "Check lending or consumer hire requirements separately from credit broking."
          ]
        ],
        "sources": [
          {
            "id": "motor",
            "label": "FCA: permissions and applications for motor dealers",
            "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/authorisation/consumer-credit-brokers/motor-dealers"
          },
          {
            "id": "broker",
            "label": "FCA: credit broker authorisation",
            "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/authorisation/consumer-credit-brokers"
          }
        ],
        "links": [],
        "id": "guide-section-1"
      },
      {
        "title": "Direct authorisation and appointed representatives",
        "paragraphs": [
          "A directly authorised dealership holds its own permissions and is responsible for complying with their scope and conditions. An appointed representative (AR) carries on the agreed activities under a principal’s responsibility. An AR arrangement is not unrestricted permission to conduct any finance activity.",
          "An introducer appointed representative (IAR) has a narrower role, limited to introductions to its principal and distributing non-real-time financial promotions. Confirm the proposed activity with the principal before relying on this route."
        ],
        "bullets": [],
        "columns": [
          "Route",
          "What it means",
          "Check before starting"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Direct authorisation",
            "Your firm applies for and holds its own permissions.",
            "The legal entity, activities, limitations and any additional permissions."
          ],
          [
            "Appointed representative (AR)",
            "A principal accepts responsibility for the agreed regulated business.",
            "A written appointment covering the proposed activities; confirm its scope with the principal."
          ],
          [
            "Introducer appointed representative (IAR)",
            "A narrower appointment for introductions and non-real-time promotions.",
            "Do not assume that helping with the wider finance process falls within the appointment."
          ]
        ],
        "sources": [
          {
            "id": "small",
            "label": "FCA: regulatory guide for credit brokers",
            "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/regulatory-guide-credit-brokers"
          },
          {
            "id": "ar",
            "label": "FCA Handbook: SUP 12.2 — appointed representatives",
            "href": "https://handbook.fca.org.uk/handbook/sup12/sup12s2"
          }
        ],
        "links": [
          [
            "/limited-permission-fca-authorisation-motor-dealers",
            "Compare limited and full permission"
          ],
          [
            "/fca-licence-car-dealers",
            "Understand FRNs and check the Register"
          ]
        ],
        "id": "guide-section-2"
      },
      {
        "title": "Fictional dealership example",
        "paragraphs": [
          "A dealer owns its stock and plans to introduce buyers to third-party vehicle finance. The FCA’s motor-dealer guidance identifies limited-permission secondary credit broking as the likely route for that activity. If the dealer also takes part-exchanges with outstanding finance, additional permissions need consideration.",
          "This example is a starting point, not a permission assessment for every dealership. Vehicle sourcing, non-owned stock and unrelated finance introductions can change the position."
        ],
        "bullets": [],
        "columns": [],
        "rows": [],
        "sources": [
          {
            "id": "motor",
            "label": "FCA: permissions and applications for motor dealers",
            "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/authorisation/consumer-credit-brokers/motor-dealers"
          }
        ],
        "links": [],
        "id": "guide-section-3"
      },
      {
        "title": "Prepare before you apply",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Before a planning meeting, bring a short description of your stock, finance introductions, partner firms and part-exchange process. Note which points are uncertain. This gives the discussion a practical starting point; a meeting does not conclusively determine permissions or authorise trading."
        ],
        "bullets": [
          "Write down who owns the vehicles and which agreements you will introduce.",
          "Record the intended lenders, brokers and any principal relationship.",
          "Describe what staff will say and do at each stage, including part-exchanges.",
          "Resolve the scope before taking on regulated activities; sending an application does not itself authorise them."
        ],
        "columns": [],
        "rows": [],
        "sources": [],
        "links": [
          [
            "/fca-application-documents-motor-dealers",
            "Prepare the application documents"
          ],
          [
            "/credit-broking-permission-motor-dealers",
            "Read the credit broking activity guide"
          ]
        ],
        "id": "guide-section-4"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Does every car dealer need FCA authorisation?",
        "answer": "No. The position depends on regulated activities, not simply being a car dealer. Finance introductions, hire and settlement assistance need separate consideration."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does using a finance broker remove the need to check permissions?",
        "answer": "No. Introducing a customer to another credit broker can itself be credit broking. Check your own role and any principal arrangement."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can we discuss our plans before applying?",
        "answer": "Yes. A planning discussion does not authorise regulated activity. Establish the required permissions or principal arrangement before starting that activity."
      }
    ],
    "related": [
      "/limited-permission-fca-authorisation-motor-dealers",
