  "/limited-permission-fca-authorisation-motor-dealers": {
    "title": "Limited Permission FCA Authorisation for Motor Dealers",
    "answer": "A motor dealer selling its own vehicles and introducing buyers to third-party finance may qualify for limited-permission secondary credit broking. That is a starting point, not a rule for every dealer. The Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) route depends on stock ownership, agreement types, sourcing, part-exchanges and other regulated activities—not simply the size of the business.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Compare the business model before choosing permissions",
        "paragraphs": [
          "“Credit broking” includes introducing customers to a lender or another credit broker. “Secondary” means that the broking supports the main goods or services business. Limited permission is still FCA authorisation; full permission is a different category, not a blanket permission for every financial service.",
          "These scenarios are starting points. The FCA’s general criteria also cover other permissions and selling in a customer’s home. Review the whole business, not just one row, and seek qualified advice where the route is unclear."
        ],
        "bullets": [],
        "columns": [
          "Business model",
          "Permission points to check"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "You own the vehicles and introduce buyers to third-party finance",
            "The FCA identifies limited-permission secondary credit broking as the likely permission."
          ],
          [
            "You own the vehicles and handle part-exchanges with outstanding finance",
            "Limited debt adjusting and debt counselling permissions may also be needed for the relevant credit activities."
          ],
          [
            "You do not own the vehicles; introductions are only for hire purchase or consumer hire; no part-exchange finance settlement",
            "The FCA identifies a limited credit broking route restricted to those agreement types."
          ],
          [
            "Same non-owned-stock model, but with part-exchange finance settlement",
            "The FCA identifies full permission for credit broking plus the relevant debt adjusting and counselling activities."
          ],
          [
            "Non-owned stock with introductions for other forms of credit",
            "Full-permission credit broking is the route identified by the FCA."
          ]
        ],
        "sources": [
          {
            "id": "motor",
            "label": "FCA: permissions and applications for motor dealers",
            "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/authorisation/consumer-credit-brokers/motor-dealers"
          },
          {
            "id": "broker",
            "label": "FCA: applying as a consumer credit broker",
            "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/authorisation/consumer-credit-brokers"
          }
        ],
        "links": [],
        "id": "guide-section-1"
      },
      {
        "title": "Vehicle sourcing and other activities",
        "paragraphs": [
          "The FCA sets separate conditions for finance introductions connected to a vehicle-sourcing service. These include whether the introduction would happen without that service and whether most revenue comes from sourcing rather than the credit introductions. Settlement of existing vehicle finance also affects the permissions to consider.",
          "Introductions unrelated to the vehicles you sell, direct lending and hiring your own vehicles need their own assessment. Do not extend a permission conclusion from one business model to another."
        ],
        "bullets": [],
        "columns": [],
        "rows": [],
        "sources": [
          {
            "id": "motor",
            "label": "FCA: permissions and applications for motor dealers",
            "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/authorisation/consumer-credit-brokers/motor-dealers"
          }
        ],
        "links": [],
        "id": "guide-section-2"
      },
      {
        "title": "Limited permission still involves ongoing obligations",
        "paragraphs": [
          "“Limited” describes a permission category; it does not mean compliance ends when the application is approved. Customer communications, applicable Consumer Duty requirements, reporting and the firm’s permission limits still matter.",
          "Prepare policies and processes proportionate to the actual business. A small dealership’s procedures can be practical and concise while still explaining who does what and how problems are handled."
        ],
        "bullets": [],
        "columns": [],
        "rows": [],
        "sources": [
          {
            "id": "small",
            "label": "FCA: regulatory guide for credit brokers",
            "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/regulatory-guide-credit-brokers"
          }
        ],
        "links": [
          [
            "/consumer-duty-motor-dealers",
            "Consumer Duty in the dealership"
          ],
          [
            "/after-fca-approval-motor-dealers",
            "What to organise after approval"
          ]
        ],
        "id": "guide-section-3"
      },
      {
        "title": "A useful preparation exercise",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Create a one-page activity map before choosing an application route. Record stock ownership, agreement types, partner firms, remuneration, part-exchanges and any separate lending or hire activity. Mark uncertainties for resolution before submission.",
          "This is a suggested preparation method, not an additional FCA form. It helps make the business plan, application answers and website consistent."
        ],
        "bullets": [],
        "columns": [
          "Fact to record",
          "Useful evidence"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Who owns each type of stock?",
            "Purchase records and any sourcing or agency agreement."
          ],
          [
            "Which finance and hire agreements are introduced?",
            "Partner terms and a sample customer journey."
          ],
          [
            "What happens to outstanding part-exchange finance?",
            "A staff walkthrough identifying who obtains figures, advises or arranges settlement."
          ],
          [
            "What other activities or permissions exist?",
            "Current Register entry, other services and any sales made in customers’ homes."
          ]
        ],
        "sources": [
          {
            "id": "motor",
            "label": "FCA: motor-dealer permissions and application guidance",
            "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/authorisation/consumer-credit-brokers/motor-dealers"
          },
          {
            "id": "broker",
            "label": "FCA: applying as a consumer credit broker",
            "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/authorisation/consumer-credit-brokers"
          }
        ],
        "links": [
          [
            "/fca-application-documents-motor-dealers",
            "Build your application document pack"
          ]
        ],
        "id": "guide-section-4"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Does a small dealership automatically qualify for limited permission?",
        "answer": "No. The activities and business model matter. Size alone does not establish eligibility."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does non-owned stock always mean full permission?",
        "answer": "No. The FCA describes a limited route for certain hire purchase and consumer hire introductions where there is no part-exchange finance settlement. Other facts can change the position."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can we add new activities after approval?",
        "answer": "Check your existing permission and any required variation before starting a new regulated activity. Do not assume the original permission covers a changed business model."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is a car finance provider the same as a motor dealer introducing finance?",
        "answer": "Not necessarily. A lender providing the money has a different role from a dealer introducing a customer. Direct lending needs a separate permission assessment; do not apply the dealer introduction example to the lender."
      }
    ],
    "related": [
      "/do-motor-dealers-need-fca-authorisation",
      "/credit-broking-permission-motor-dealers",
      "/fca-application-documents-motor-dealers"
    ],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "motor",
        "label": "FCA: permissions and applications for motor dealers",
        "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/authorisation/consumer-credit-brokers/motor-dealers"
      },
      {
        "id": "broker",
        "label": "FCA: credit broker authorisation",
        "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/authorisation/consumer-credit-brokers"
      },
      {
        "id": "small",
        "label": "FCA: regulatory guide for credit brokers",
        "href": "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/regulatory-guide-credit-brokers"
      }
    ],
    "group": "fca",
    "finance": true,
    "reviewed": "2026-11-01",
    "description": "Compare limited and full FCA permission scenarios for motor dealers, including owned stock, vehicle sourcing and part-exchanges with outstanding finance.",
    "seoTitle": "Limited vs Full FCA Permission: Motor Dealers | 009 Compliance",
    "modified": "2026-11-12",
