  "/why-fca-applications-get-delayed": {
    "title": "Why FCA Applications Get Delayed: A Motor Dealer Guide",
    "answer": "FCA applications can be delayed by missing information, inconsistent documents or questions about how the business will operate. Separate preparation time, formal assessment periods and the FCA’s operating targets. None is an average approval time or a promise that your dealership will be authorised by a particular date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Separate preparation from FCA assessment",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Allow for gathering information and resolving permission questions before submitting. That preparation period is distinct from the FCA’s assessment of the submitted application. A six-month assistance subscription is also a commercial commitment, not an FCA decision timetable.",
          "The FCA may ask follow-up questions, request documents or hold a call or discussion. Approval depends on the application meeting the applicable standards."
        ],
        "bullets": [],
        "columns": [
          "Timing measure",
          "What it means"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Formal assessment periods",
            "The FCA’s consumer-credit application notes describe the legal determination period as the earlier of six months from receipt of a completed application or twelve months from receipt of an incomplete application."
          ],
          [
            "Current operating target",
            "The FCA’s 2026/27 Q1 metrics set a 95% target of four months for complete new Part 4A authorisation applications and ten months for incomplete applications. Part 4A is the authorisation route for permission to carry on regulated activities."
          ],
          [
            "Your application",
            "A target is not an average, an approval rate or a guaranteed decision date. Preparation before submission is separate."
          ]
        ],
