  "/consumer-duty-motor-dealers": {
    "title": "Consumer Duty for Motor Dealers: Outcomes and Practical Checks",
    "answer": "Consumer Duty requires firms in scope to act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers. For a motor dealer, the practical focus includes the finance service offered, fair value, customer understanding and support. Responsibilities depend on the firm’s role and ability to influence outcomes; they are not identical for a dealer, broker and lender.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "The four outcomes in a dealership",
        "paragraphs": [
          "These examples show possible evidence and actions. They are suggested ways to assess outcomes, not a mandatory list of documents or a complete compliance assessment."
        ],
        "bullets": [],
        "columns": [
          "Outcome",
          "Dealership example",
          "Evidence and possible action"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Products and services",
            "Consider whether the finance introduction service fits its intended customers.",
            "Review the customer journey, partner information and instances where the process did not fit customer needs."
          ],
          [
            "Price and value",
            "Consider the relationship between what customers pay and the benefits received.",
            "Review relevant charges, remuneration and value information; do not judge value only by a low monthly payment."
          ],
          [
            "Consumer understanding",
            "Help customers understand the dealer’s role and the information relevant to their decision.",
            "Check customer questions and sample communications; improve wording that repeatedly causes confusion."
          ],
          [
            "Consumer support",
            "Provide support that meets customers’ needs through the relevant journey.",
            "Review delays, contact difficulties and support needs; allocate and follow up corrective actions."
          ]
        ],
        "sources": [
          {
            "id": "duty",
            "label": "FCA Handbook: PRIN 2A — Consumer Duty",
            "href": "https://handbook.fca.org.uk/handbook/prin2a"
          }
        ],
        "links": [],
        "id": "guide-section-1"
      },
      {
        "title": "Match responsibilities to your role",
        "paragraphs": [
          "A dealership distributing a lender’s product has a different role from the lender that designs it. That difference does not mean the dealership can ignore outcomes it influences. Identify what the firm controls, what it relies on partners for and how concerns are escalated.",
          "The Duty’s cross-cutting obligations include acting in good faith, avoiding foreseeable harm and enabling and supporting retail customers to pursue their financial objectives. Apply the relevant rules to the actual service and customer journey."
        ],
        "bullets": [],
        "columns": [],
        "rows": [],
        "sources": [
          {
            "id": "duty",
            "label": "FCA Handbook: PRIN 2A — Consumer Duty",
            "href": "https://handbook.fca.org.uk/handbook/prin2a"
          }
        ],
        "links": [],
        "id": "guide-section-2"
      },
      {
        "title": "Turn observations into action",
        "paragraphs": [
          "A useful monitoring record sets out the issue, the evidence, which customers may be affected, the owner, the action and whether it worked. Use proportionate evidence from the business rather than collecting figures with no purpose.",
          "For example, repeated questions about who provides finance may indicate unclear role wording. Review the wording, brief staff and check whether customers understand it better afterwards. This is an illustrative improvement cycle, not a claim that one test proves compliance."
        ],
        "bullets": [],
        "columns": [],
        "rows": [],
        "sources": [],
        "links": [],
        "id": "guide-section-3"
      },
      {
        "title": "Support vulnerable customers",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Build a practical route for staff to recognise and respond to support needs. Consider communication preferences, time to understand information and escalation to an appropriate person. Avoid treating vulnerability as a single label that explains every customer’s needs.",
          "The governing body’s review of retail customer outcomes is required at least annually under PRIN 2A.8.4R for firms within its application. A monthly internal check may help your business, but it is not a universal monthly reporting requirement under that rule."
        ],
        "bullets": [],
        "columns": [],
        "rows": [],
        "sources": [
          {
            "id": "duty",
            "label": "FCA Handbook: PRIN 2A — Consumer Duty",
            "href": "https://handbook.fca.org.uk/handbook/prin2a"
          },
          {
            "id": "governance",
            "label": "FCA Handbook: PRIN 2A.8 — governance and culture",
            "href": "https://handbook.fca.org.uk/handbook/prin2a/prin2as8"
          }
        ],
        "links": [
          [
            "/website-compliance-motor-dealers",
            "Check customer-facing website content"
          ],
          [
            "/fca-compliance-motor-dealers-guide",
            "Organise the wider compliance process"
          ]
        ],
        "id": "guide-section-4"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Does a small dealership have no Consumer Duty responsibilities?",
        "answer": "Size alone does not remove responsibilities. Check whether the firm and activity are in scope and apply the requirements proportionately to its role and customers."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does fair value simply mean the lowest monthly payment?",
        "answer": "No. Price and value concerns the relationship between the price paid and the benefits received, taking account of the relevant product or service."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is a written policy enough?",
        "answer": "A policy should be supported by how the business operates, monitors outcomes and addresses problems. A document alone does not show that customers receive good outcomes."
      }
    ],
    "related": [
      "/website-compliance-motor-dealers",
      "/after-fca-approval-motor-dealers",
      "/fca-compliance-motor-dealers-guide"
    ],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "duty",
        "label": "FCA Handbook: PRIN 2A — Consumer Duty",
        "href": "https://handbook.fca.org.uk/handbook/prin2a"
      },
